Article 2

The implementation of protection for women victims of violence is carried out based on the principles of:
a. respect for human rights;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination; and d. the best interests of victims

Article 3

The implementation of child protection is based on Pancasila and grounded in the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia as well as the basic principles of the Convention on the Rights of the Child, including:
a. collecting data and information about women and children victims of violence;
b. conducting education on anti-violence values towards women and children;
c. socialization of laws and regulations related to the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence; d. providing facilities required for victim recovery, including:
1. skilled and professional personnel;
2. service centers, safe houses; and
3. other infrastructure and facilities required for victim recovery. e. monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence.

Article 3O

The government and other state institutions are obliged and responsible to provide special protection to children in emergency situations, children in conflict with the law, children from minority and isolated groups, children exploited economically and/or sexually, children who are trafficked, children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substances (napza) abuse, children who are victims of kidnapping, sale and trafficking, children who are victims of violence both physical and/or mental, children with disabilities, and children who are victims of mistreatment and neglect.

Article 31 Children in emergency situations as referred to in Article 3O consist of:
a. children who become refugees;
b. children who are victims of unrest;
c. children who are victims of natural disasters; andd. children in armed conflict situations.

