Article 3O

The government and other state institutions are obliged and responsible to provide special protection to children in emergency situations, children in conflict with the law, children from minority and isolated groups, children exploited economically and/or sexually, children who are trafficked, children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substances (napza) abuse, children who are victims of kidnapping, sale and trafficking, children who are victims of violence both physical and/or mental, children with disabilities, and children who are victims of mistreatment and neglect.

Article 31 Children in emergency situations as referred to in Article 3O consist of:
a. children who become refugees;
b. children who are victims of unrest;
c. children who are victims of natural disasters; andd. children in armed conflict situations.

Article 35

1. Special protection for children in conflict with the law as referred to in Article 3O includes children in conflict with the law and children who are victims of criminal acts, which is the obligation and responsibility of the government and society.
2. Special protection for children in conflict with the law as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented through: a. treatment of children in a humane manner in accordance with the dignity and rights of children;
b. provision of special child assistance officers from an early stage; c. provision of special facilities and infrastructure;
d. imposition of appropriate sanctions for the best interests of the child;
e. continuous monitoring and recording of the development of children in conflict with the law;
f. provision of guarantees to maintain relationships with parents or family; andg. protection from identity disclosure through the mass media and to avoid labeling.
3. Special protection for children who are victims of criminal acts as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented through: a. rehabilitation efforts, both in institutions and outside institutions; b. protection efforts from identity disclosure through the mass media and to avoid labeling;
c. provision of safety guarantees for victim witnesses and expert witnesses, whether physical, mental, or social; andd. provision of accessibility to obtain information regarding case developments.

